nd  actually it's quite patronizing friend  peeks to say that they didn't really  know what they were voting well let's be  honest a lot of water has passed under  the bridge to resume lost by two hundred  and thirty-odd votes we are now in a  mess it's a constitutional crisis it's  not clear that  is a majority for any deal in fact so  what we can't do is crash out which is  painful I hope a deal emerges and it's a  soft brexit but it does seem to me that  there is a risk that we upset both  leavers and remain errs if basically  Westminster hatches the deal and signs  and delivers it and many people think I  didn't actually vote for this so that  reason that you've got to have a final  say the British people has to RIT has  got to resolve this and I've said this  before Sophie you cannot argue that you  undermined democracy with more democracy  it's just not the way that some great  nations like ours establish themselves  but some people would feel that it is -  my own democracy that it's their voices  being ignored are you not worried about  some of the consequences for the country  if we have to go through another  referendum before when the first one was  sowed devices look we've seen the  division in the country we see it  outside Westminster with the way that  some members of the public often on the  hard right are behaving towards  parliamentarians we see it in families  that are divided across this country yes  of course we have to bring this to an  end but it seems to me that the way to  bring it to an end is to present the  British people no longer with this leave  and we don't understand what the terms  of leaving are this time we understand  exactly what the terms of leaving are  when we get that deal the Parliament can  sign off or indeed we can remain within  the European Union which is the option I  prefer let's be clear on that because  we've looked into the abyss and decided  sorry not for us okay and you said and a  little bit earlier in the interview and  that you hope a deal is done does that  mean that there is a brexit deal that  you could vote for well certainly I  would prefer a soft brexit somewhere  like Norway to Tereza males watch deal  that got us nowhere and they might  reject it was on the table  it's the terms under which it was put I  am very clear and I'm writing in the  observer today that I could only vote  for it on the basis that there was a  final say referendum because if you're  someone let's say in a Labour heartland  constituency you thought you were  getting an end to free movement and  politicians have taken that off the  table you thought you weren't gonna be  paying into the money into the EU and  politicians are now saying we are gonna  still be paying money into the EU and  and worse you're now subject to all the  rules of the European Union without a  say then my god surely we've got to ask  the British people if they're up for  that that's only fair  so of course economically it would be  less damaging but my view is look let's  stay in the European Union and actually  have an influence on free movement  actually have an influence on the rules  and actually have an influence on  bureaucracy red tape and monies that's  my view let's see what the British  people think and how would you describe  Labor's current position on brexit well  look I think we've got an open goal here  and I'd like the Labour Party to go  through the door and score the goal and  the only way of doing that it seems to  me is to get us into a place where we  are preparing for that final say  referendum that has to take place he's  hitting the crossbar well he's moving  the goalposts and I I've been very clear  on that you know we we now have seen  that the deal has not gone through that  a vote of confidence has not been  successful as people like me predicted  back in September last year it seems to  me there's no point continuing with  votes of no confidence you know throwing  darts but actually is missing a board  elections going to happen I think an  election is extremely unlikely because  members of the DUP are not going to put  Jeremy Corbyn who has been a long term  friend of shim fain into number 10 let's  be clear and even  Dominic grieve and a Subaru are not  saying that they are going to vote no  confidence in a Conservative government  which they prefer to Jeremy Corbyn so I  don't see any prospects of a general  election no we've what we've got to do  is get around what the alternative deal  is but be clear that we should set in  place the timetable now to get that  second referendum that's a final say  referendum and let's be clear on this  the only way we're now going to get a  brexit is if the labour party whips for  it so the question is is the Labour  Party going to whip for Teresa Mays  brexit or is the Labour Party going to  get clear on the options for the British  people the best options of the British  people and my view the best options for  the British people is now to remain  within the european union jeremy  corbyn's always talked about the  importance of party democracy about  listening to the party members the  conference motion were saying that they  want to push for a general election if  you can't get that then a second  referendum is her on the table now as  you've said a general election seems  unlikely you've had the confidence  version vote and lost so why do you  think it is that the party isn't  explicitly backing a second referendum  and do you think Jeremy Corbyn is going  back on some of those very strong words  he's used in the past about the  importance of listening to membership  well I think kirsten was very clear  yesterday the moment has arrived the  moment has arrived for clarity and for  leadership and for those of us that have  campaigned for a final say people's vote  we are absolutely clear that requires  the labour party to whip for not done it  well look I think the jeremy corbyn's  i think that jeremy has been he